WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
The first film followed a body as it pushed back against warming.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.620
Do hearts, leaves, roots, and cells all use that same loop?

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.840
Keep the same colors while the parts change.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.360
A gold badge marks the condition each case helps protect.

5
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:21.913
Start with the heart during exercise.

6
00:00:23.593 --> 00:00:25.853
Commands from the brain speed it as movement begins.

7
00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:30.693
Sensors in working muscles and blood vessels add information.

8
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:35.193
The faster heart moves more blood past those muscles.

9
00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:41.373
Exercise control combines advance commands from the brain with feedback from

10
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:42.373
sensors.

11
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:45.283
Now move the gold range badge to a leaf.

12
00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:50.903
Dry air makes water vapor leave faster through stomata, the leaf's small pores.

13
00:00:52.543 --> 00:00:55.003
Guard cells can narrow each pore and slow that loss.

14
00:00:56.223 --> 00:00:58.383
But the pore also admits carbon dioxide.

15
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:04.547
Predict what happens to carbon dioxide entry when the guard cells narrow the pore.

16
00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:09.087
Does only water movement change, or do both movements slow?

17
00:01:10.387 --> 00:01:12.107
Watch the two arrows while you decide.

18
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.500
Both movements slow.

19
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.600
Fewer water-vapor particles escape, helping the leaf keep water.

20
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.480
Fewer carbon-dioxide particles enter, which is the tradeoff.

21
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.920
One response can protect one condition while limiting another process.

22
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.950
A root takes a slower path.

23
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:43.750
When water is uneven in soil, the root can curve as it grows toward wetter ground.

24
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.530
This is hydrotropism.

25
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:53.150
It supports water supply, but biologists usually call it a response, not a complete

26
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.150
feedback loop.

27
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.150
A single cell uses its boundary.

28
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.610
The selectively permeable membrane lets some substances cross and holds others back.

29
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:09.450
Passive transport follows a concentration gradient without cellular energy.

30
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.990
Active transport spends cellular energy to move against that gradient.

31
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.650
Together, these routes keep the inside different.

32
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.320
Each case helps keep something steady, but by a different route.

33
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.120
The heart changes flow, guard cells change a pore, and membranes control what

34
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.000
crosses. Roots support water supply by changing growth direction.

35
00:02:35.083 --> 00:02:38.503
The goal is shared, but the diagrams are not identical.

36
00:02:39.903 --> 00:02:44.163
Body temperature fits the full sensor, control center, and effector map.

37
00:02:45.943 --> 00:02:47.323
Exercise adds advance commands.

38
00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:51.703
Hydrotropism is not usually called a complete loop.

39
00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:55.203
A membrane needs no brain-like controller.

40
00:02:56.333 --> 00:03:00.873
The text now adds what the pictures skipped: the heart's two nerves and what

41
00:03:00.873 --> 00:03:02.073
complicates leaf pores.

42
00:03:03.453 --> 00:03:07.453
Then real heart-rate data show how one return unfolds over time.
